{"command": "cat /testbed/specs/001-transactions/spec.md", "exit_code": 0, "stderr": "", "stdout": "# Feature Specification: Tagged Cache (TaggedCache)\n\n**Feature Branch**: `001-transactions`\n\n**Created**: 2026-10-17\n\n**Status**: Draft (updated 2026-10-17 for milestone 2 + round-2 clarification)\n\n**Input**: User description: \"Build cachetools.tagged.TaggedCache(maxsize), using the\nexisting cachetools.LRUCache for bounded storage and preserving all existing\ncachetools APIs. R01 put/get/copying/tags validation; R02 invalidate/recency/\neviction hygiene; R03 maxsize validation and atomic replacement. Claim-A\ncandidate extension (variant put_no_recency_refresh, 2026-09-21): R02\nextension - overwriting an existing key via put refreshes its LRU recency, making\nit the most-recently-used entry. Milestone 2 (retaining R01-R03): R04\ninvalidate_many(tags, mode='any'); R05 resize(maxsize) with maxsize property;\nR06 generator tags consumed once + repeated-get/put detachment. Conflicting\nevidence: an old generated design note ('empty all-tags matches every key and\nresizing may flush the cache') is superseded by R04/R05. Claim-A candidate\nextensions, round 2 (2026-09-22): R05 clarification - resize must preserve the\nabsolute expiry of surviving entries (R07/R08 define the expiry as entry\nstate).\"\n\n## User Scenarios & Testing *(mandatory)*\n\n### User Story 1 - Store and Retrieve Tagged Entries (Priority: P1)\n\nA developer creates a bounded `TaggedCache(maxsize)` and stores values under\nhashable keys with string tags. Retrieval by key returns a detached deep copy\nof the stored value; a missing key raises `KeyError`. Values are copied on\ninput as well, so later mutation of the caller's object does not change the\ncached content. Tags are deduplicated; a bare string or a non-string tag entry\nis rejected with `ValueError` before any existing state changes.\n\n**Why this priority**: Core value of the feature - safe, tagged, bounded\nstorage. Without it nothing else works.\n\n**Independent Test**: Create a cache, put a value with tags, get it back and\nassert equality plus detachment (mutate the returned object, re-get, value\nunchanged); assert `KeyError` for a missing key; assert `ValueError` for\n`tags=\"x\"` and `tags=[1]` and that state is unchanged after rejection.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** an empty `TaggedCache(2)`, **When** `put('a', 1, ['x'])` then\n   `get('a')` returns `1`.\n2. **Given** a cached mutable value, **When** the caller mutates the original\n   object after `put`, **Then** `get` returns the value as stored at insertion\n   (copied on input).\n3. **Given** a cached value, **When** the caller mutates the object returned by\n   `get`, **Then** a subsequent `get` returns the unmutated stored value\n   (detached deep copy).\n4. **Given** any key not present, **When** `get(key)` is called, **Then**\n   `KeyError` is raised.\n5. **Given** a cache with existing entries, **When** `put(key, value, tags)` is\n   called with a bare string as `tags` or a non-string tag entry, **Then**\n   `ValueError` is raised and no existing entries, tags, or recency change.\n6. **Given** `put('a', 1, ['x', 'x', 'y'])`, **When** the entry is inspected\n   via `invalidate`, **Then** the entry is tagged exactly once per distinct\n   tag (`invalidate('x') == 1`, `invalidate('y') == 1`).\n\n---\n\n### User Story 2 - Invalidate by Tag and Correct LRU Eviction (Priority: P2)\n\nA developer removes all currently cached entries carrying a given tag in one\ncall and learns how many were removed. Replacing a key removes its old tag\nmemberships. When the cache is full, the least-recently-used entry is evicted\nand eviction leaves no stale tag memberships that could affect later reuse of\nthe evicted key. `get` refreshes LRU recency. `len(cache)` counts live\nentries.\n\n**Why this priority**: Tag-based bulk removal and eviction correctness are the\nsecond core value; they depend on Story 1's storage but are independently\ntestable.\n\n**Independent Test**: Fill a small cache, call `invalidate(tag)` and assert the\nreturned count and that only tagged entries were removed; put past capacity and\nassert the LRU victim was evicted and that re-putting the same key with new\ntags does not resurrect old memberships.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** entries tagged `'x'` and `'y'`, **When** `invalidate('x')`,\n   **Then** it returns the number of removed entries and only those entries are\n   gone; `invalidate` of an unknown tag returns `0`.\n2. **Given** a key stored with tags `['old']`, **When** the same key is\n   re-put with tags `['new']`, **Then** `invalidate('old')` returns `0` and\n   `get(key)` returns the new value (old memberships removed).\n3. **Given** a full cache, **When** a new entry is put, **Then** the\n   least-recently-used entry is evicted, `len` stays at `maxsize`, and a later\n   `put` of the evicted key with different tags does not carry the old tags.\n4. **Given** a full cache where one entry was just `get`-accessed, **When** a\n   new entry is put, **Then** the accessed entry survives and a different LRU\n   entry is evicted (get refreshes recency).\n5. **Given** any sequence of puts/gets/invalidates, **When** `len(cache)` is\n   called, **Then** it equals the number of live entries and never exceeds\n   `maxsize`.\n\n---\n\n### User Story 3 - Configuration and Replacement Safety (Priority: P3)\n\nA developer constructs the cache with a positive integer `maxsize` (booleans\nare rejected) and relies on replacement being atomic: invalid replacement\ninput (bad tags, bad value handling) leaves the previously stored value, tags,\nand recency exactly as they were. Every value costs exactly one capacity slot\nregardless of object size.\n\n**Why this priority**: Guards against data corruption and misconfiguration;\nimportant but exercised less frequently than put/get/invalidate.\n\n**Independent Test**: Assert `TaggedCache(0)`, `TaggedCache(-1)`,\n`TaggedCache(True)`, `TaggedCache(2.0)` raise `ValueError`; put a valid entry,\nattempt `put` with invalid tags, and assert the old value/tags/recency are\nunchanged; put `maxsize` large objects and confirm the (maxsize+1)th evicts the\nLRU entry (one slot per value).\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** a non-positive, non-integer, or boolean `maxsize`, **When** the\n   cache is constructed, **Then** `ValueError` is raised.\n2. **Given** a key with value `v1` and tags `['t']`, **When** `put(key, v2,\n   ['bad'])` (non-string tag) fails, **Then** `get(key)` still returns a copy\n   of `v1`, `invalidate('t')` still returns `1`, and the entry's recency is\n   unchanged.\n3. **Given** `maxsize` entries of very different object sizes, **When** one\n   more entry is put, **Then** exactly the LRU entry is evicted (each value\n   occupies one slot regardless of size).\n\n### User Story 4 - Multi-Tag Invalidation, Resizing, and Lazy Tags (Priority: P2)\n\nA developer invalidates entries matching several tags in one call, choosing\nwhether to remove entries carrying ANY of the supplied tags or only entries\ncarrying ALL of them. The developer can change the cache capacity at runtime:\nshrinking evicts least-recently-used entries while growing never evicts, and a\nfailed resize leaves the cache exactly as it was. Tags may be supplied as\ngenerators (lazy iterables) and are consumed exactly once. Returned values\nstay detached across repeated `get` and `put` calls, including nested lists\nand dicts.\n\n**Why this priority**: Bulk invalidation and runtime resizing extend the core\ntagged-cache value to operational use; they build on Stories 1-3 but are\nindependently testable.\n\n**Independent Test**: Put entries with overlapping tag sets, call\n`invalidate_many` in both modes and assert the removed keys and returned\ncounts; assert empty tag requests remove nothing; assert invalid mode or\ninvalid tags raise `ValueError` with state unchanged. Fill a cache, shrink it\nand assert the LRU victims were evicted with surviving order preserved; grow it\nand assert nothing was evicted; assert a failed `resize` (e.g., `True`, `0`,\n`-1`, `2.5`) leaves capacity and all state unchanged and that the `maxsize`\nproperty reports the current capacity. Pass a generator as `tags` and assert\nit is consumed exactly once and that repeated `get`/`put` never alias the\nstored value.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** entries with tags `{'a','b'}`, `{'b'}`, and `{'c'}`, **When**\n   `invalidate_many(['a','b'])` (mode `'any'`), **Then** the first two entries\n   are removed, the third survives, and the return value is the number of\n   distinct removed keys (`2`).\n2. **Given** the same entries, **When** `invalidate_many(['a','b'], mode='all')`,\n   **Then** only the entry carrying both `'a'` and `'b'` is removed and the\n   return value is `1`.\n3. **Given** any non-empty cache, **When** `invalidate_many(())` or\n   `invalidate_many([], mode='all')` is called, **Then** zero entries are\n   removed in either mode and state is unchanged (empty requested tags never\n   match; an empty `'all'` request does NOT match every key).\n4. **Given** a cache, **When** `invalidate_many` is called with an invalid\n   `mode` (not `'any'`/`'all'`) or invalid tags (bare string, non-string\n   entry), **Then** `ValueError` is raised BEFORE any mutation and state is\n   unchanged.\n5. **Given** a full cache of `maxsize` entries, **When** `resize(smaller)` is\n   called, **Then** the least-recently-used entries are evicted until the live\n   count equals the new capacity, surviving entries keep their relative LRU\n   order, and the `maxsize` property reports the new capacity.\n6. **Given** a cache, **When** `resize(larger)` is called, **Then** no entry is\n   evicted, the `maxsize` property reports the new capacity, and the cache can\n   then hold up to the new capacity.\n7. **Given** a cache with entries, **When** `resize(True)`, `resize(0)`,\n   `resize(-1)`, or `resize(2.5)` is called, **Then** `ValueError` is raised\n   and capacity, entries, tags, and recency are all unchanged.\n8. **Given** a cache, **When** `put` is called with a generator as `tags`,\n   **Then** the generator is consumed exactly once, the entry is stored with\n   the yielded (deduplicated) tags, and a generator that raises mid-iteration\n   or a bare string is rejected with `ValueError` before any state change.\n9. **Given** a nested value (list/dict), **When** it is `get`-retrieved\n   repeatedly and `put`-replaced repeatedly, **Then** every returned object is\n   a fresh detached deep copy: mutating any returned object or the original\n   input never changes the stored value, and two successive `get`s are not the\n   same object.\n\n### Edge Cases\n\n- `put` with `tags=()` (default) or an empty iterable: entry stored with no\ntags; `invalidate(anything)` does not remove it.\n- `put` with a tag iterable containing duplicates: stored once per distinct\ntag (deduplicated); order of first occurrence preserved for determinism.\n- `put` with an infinite/lazy iterable of tags: iteration is finite by\n  contract; validation must complete before state change (a bare string is\n  rejected rather than iterated character-by-character).\n- Re-putting an evicted key: behaves as a fresh insertion with the new tags\n  only (no stale memberships from before eviction).\n- `invalidate` with a tag that no live entry carries: returns `0`, state\n  unchanged.\n- Replacement of a key with the same value but different tags: old tag\n  memberships removed, new memberships applied, recency refreshed (R02\n  extension).\n- `maxsize` given as `bool` (`True`/`False`): rejected (`ValueError`) even\n  though `bool` is an `int` subclass.\n- `maxsize` given as a float or other non-int: rejected (`ValueError`).\n\n- `invalidate_many` with duplicate tags in the request: the request is\n  deduplicated for matching (a tag listed twice does not change which entries\n  match or the returned count).\n- `invalidate_many` in mode `'all'` with a single tag: removes exactly the\n  entries carrying that tag, identical to `invalidate(tag)`.\n- `resize` to the same capacity: valid no-op; entries, tags, and LRU order\n  unchanged.\n- `resize` while the cache is empty: valid; only the capacity changes.\n- Shrink below the live count where some entries share tags: eviction is by\n  LRU recency only; tag membership of evicted entries is fully removed (no\n  stale memberships, consistent with FR-007).\n- Generator tags: the same generator object must not be re-consumed by the\n  cache after `put` returns; the cache stores only the materialized tag set.\n- Expiry (forward reference): when entry expiry state exists (defined by\n  R07/R08, not in the current milestone input), `resize` MUST preserve each\n  surviving entry's absolute expiry; dropping expiries (making entries\n  immortal) or recomputing them from the resize time is a violation (R05\n  clarification, round 2).\n\n## Requirements *(mandatory)*\n\n### Functional Requirements\n\n- **FR-001** (R01): `TaggedCache(maxsize)` MUST accept a `maxsize` that is a\n  positive integer (bool excluded); all other values MUST raise `ValueError`\n  (R03).\n- **FR-002** (R01): `put(key, value, tags=())` MUST insert or replace an entry\n  for a hashable key; the stored value MUST be an independent deep copy of the\n  input value.\n- **FR-003** (R01): `get(key)` MUST return a detached deep copy of the stored\n  value, and MUST raise `KeyError` when the key is absent.\n- **FR-004** (R01): `tags` MUST be a finite iterable of strings; a bare string\n  MUST be rejected and non-string entries MUST be rejected, both with\n  `ValueError`, in both cases BEFORE any existing cache state (entries, tags,\n  recency) is modified; duplicate tags MUST be deduplicated.\n- **FR-005** (R02): `invalidate(tag)` MUST remove exactly the currently cached\n  entries carrying `tag` and MUST return the number removed.\n- **FR-006** (R02): Replacing an existing key MUST remove its previous tag\n  memberships before applying the new ones.\n- **FR-007** (R02): LRU eviction MUST remove the evicted entry's tag\n  memberships so later reuse of the same key carries no stale memberships.\n- **FR-008** (R02): `get(key)` on a hit MUST refresh that entry's LRU recency.\n- **FR-009** (R02): `len(cache)` MUST count live entries and MUST never exceed\n  `maxsize`.\n- **FR-010** (R02 extension, Claim-A, variant `put_no_recency_refresh`,\n  2026-09-21): Overwriting an existing key via `put` MUST refresh its LRU\n  recency, making it the most-recently-used entry.\n- **FR-011** (R03): A failed replacement (invalid input) MUST leave the\n  previous value, tags, and recency unchanged.\n- **FR-012** (R03): Every value MUST occupy exactly one capacity slot\n  regardless of object size.\n- **FR-013** (preservation): All existing public `cachetools` APIs MUST retain\n  their current behavior; the new cache MUST be built on the existing\n  `cachetools.LRUCache` for bounded storage; upstream tests MUST remain\n  unchanged and passing.\n- **FR-014** (R04): `invalidate_many(tags, mode='any')` MUST remove the union\n  of live entries carrying any of the supplied tags when `mode='any'`, and\n  only live entries carrying every supplied tag when `mode='all'`; it MUST\n  return the number of distinct removed keys.\n- **FR-015** (R04): An empty requested tag set MUST remove zero entries in\n  either mode (in particular, an empty `'all'` request MUST NOT match every\n  key) and MUST return `0`.\n- **FR-016** (R04): `invalidate_many` MUST reject an invalid `mode` (anything\n  other than `'any'` or `'all'`) and invalid tags (bare string, non-string\n  entries) with `ValueError` BEFORE any mutation; `invalidate(tag)` MUST\n  remain compatible (same semantics as `invalidate_many([tag], mode='any')`).\n- **FR-017** (R05): `resize(maxsize)` MUST validate its argument first as a\n  positive integer excluding `bool` (else `ValueError`); shrinking MUST evict\n  least-recently-used entries until the live count equals the new capacity\n  while preserving surviving entries' relative LRU order; growing MUST not\n  evict; a failed resize MUST preserve all state (capacity, entries, tags,\n  recency).\n- **FR-018** (R05): The `maxsize` property MUST report the cache's current\n  capacity (initial value from construction; updated by successful `resize`).\n- **FR-019** (R05 clarification, Claim-A round 2, 2026-09-22): Preserving a\n  surviving entry across `resize` MUST preserve its absolute expiry (R07/R08\n  define the expiry as entry state); a resize that drops expiries (making\n  entries immortal) or recomputes them from the resize time MUST NOT be\n  accepted as satisfying R05. Expiry itself is out of scope until R07/R08 are\n  added; this clause constrains any future expiry state.\n- **FR-020** (R06): Tags MUST be accepted as any finite iterable, including\n  generators, and MUST be consumed exactly once; the cache MUST store only the\n  materialized (deduplicated) tag set, never the original iterable.\n- **FR-021** (R06): Returned values MUST remain detached across repeated\n  `get`s and `put`s, including nested lists and dicts; no returned object\n  (or object nested within one) may alias the stored value or any other\n  returned object.\n\n### Key Entities\n\n- **TaggedCache**: A bounded cache instance configured by `maxsize`; holds\n  live entries; enforces LRU eviction and tag bookkeeping.\n- **Entry**: A live mapping of one hashable key to one deep-copied value and a\n  set of distinct string tags; occupies exactly one capacity slot; has an LRU\n  recency position.\n- **Tag**: A distinct string identifying an entry for bulk invalidation; an\n  entry may carry zero or more tags; a tag may be carried by many entries.\n\n## Success Criteria *(mandatory)*\n\n### Measurable Outcomes\n\n- **SC-001**: 100% of the milestone's acceptance scenarios (User Stories 1-3)\n  pass as executable tests, including the public acceptance suite\n  (`acceptance_public.py`) and project-authored tests, with zero failures.\n- **SC-002**: 100% of existing upstream tests pass unchanged after the feature\n  is added (no regressions in existing `cachetools` APIs).\n- **SC-003**: For every rejection case (invalid `maxsize`, bare-string tags,\n  non-string tag entries, invalid replacement input), 100% leave observable\n  cache state (entries, tags, recency, length) exactly as before the call.\n- **SC-004**: After any sequence of at least 1000 mixed operations (put,\n  replace, get, invalidate, eviction) on a small cache, `len` equals the live\n  entry count, never exceeds `maxsize`, and no evicted key's old tags affect a\n  later re-put of that key (0 stale memberships).\n- **SC-005**: Retrieval isolation: mutating either the input object or a\n  returned object never changes the stored value (verified for nested\n  container values, not only scalars).\n- **SC-006**: `invalidate_many` removes exactly the entries described by the\n  requested tag set and mode in 100% of test cases (union for `'any'`,\n  intersection for `'all'`), returns the exact distinct removed-key count,\n  removes zero entries for empty requests in both modes, and leaves state\n  unchanged for 100% of invalid mode/tag inputs.\n- **SC-007**: `resize` succeeds for 100% of valid positive non-bool integer\n  capacities: shrinking evicts exactly the least-recently-used entries (no\n  other evictions), growing evicts none, surviving LRU order is preserved in\n  100% of cases, the `maxsize` property always reports the current capacity,\n  and 100% of invalid inputs (bool, zero, negative, float, non-numeric) leave\n  all state unchanged.\n- **SC-008**: A generator passed as tags is consumed exactly once in 100% of\n  cases (verified with an instrumented generator), and repeated `get`/`put`\n  cycles (>= 10 per key) produce 100% detached copies for nested list/dict\n  values (mutation of any returned object never changes stored state).\n\n## Assumptions\n\n- **A1**: \"Detached deep copy\" means a copy deep enough that mutating nested\n  container structures in the input or returned object does not affect the\n  stored value; scalar values are equal-valued copies.\n- **A2**: `put` returns no value (consistent with existing `cachetools`\n  caches); the observable contract is via `get`, `len`, and `invalidate`.\n- **A3**: Tag validation is performed by materializing the tag iterable into a\n  finite sequence of strings; a bare string is rejected explicitly (not\n  treated as an iterable of characters); an infinite iterable is out of\n  contract (tags must be finite).\n- **A4**: Recency ordering follows standard LRU semantics: `put` (insert or\n  replace) and `get` (hit) mark an entry most-recently-used; eviction removes\n  the least-recently-used entry. This includes the Claim-A extension\n  (FR-010): replacing an existing key refreshes its recency.\n- **A5**: Invalid `maxsize` values include `0`, negatives, booleans, floats,\n  and non-numeric types; all raise `ValueError` at construction.\n- **A6**: The feature is delivered as an additive module\n  (`src/cachetools/tagged.py`) with no changes to existing modules' behavior\n  and no new dependencies.\n- **A7**: `invalidate` accepts a single tag (string) and remains compatible\n  with `invalidate_many([tag], mode='any')`; bulk/multi-tag invalidation is\n  provided by `invalidate_many` (R04).\n- **A8**: The Claim-A candidate extension is treated as an active requirement\n  (FR-010) per the milestone input; the variant label\n  `put_no_recency_refresh` is noted for traceability only and does NOT\n  override FR-010 (i.e., recency refresh on overwrite IS required).\n- **A9**: The conflicting old design note ('empty all-tags matches every key\n  and resizing may flush the cache') is superseded by R04/R05 and MUST NOT be\n  relied upon; specifically, empty requested tags remove zero entries and\n  resizing never flushes the cache.\n- **A10**: The R05 round-2 clarification (absolute-expiry preservation) is\n  recorded as FR-019 and is binding for any implementation that introduces\n  expiry state (R07/R08); since the current milestone input defines no expiry\n  behavior, no expiry is implemented or tested in this milestone.\n- **A11**: `invalidate_many` deduplicates the requested tag set for matching\n  (duplicates in the request do not change the match set or the returned\ncount); the returned count is always the number of distinct removed keys.\n\n## Requirement Traceability\n\n| Milestone input | Spec requirement(s) | Notes |\n|-----------------|---------------------|-------|\n| R01 | FR-002, FR-003, FR-004 | put/get/copying/tags |\n| R02 | FR-005, FR-006, FR-007, FR-008, FR-009 | invalidate, replace, eviction, recency, len |\n| R02 extension (Claim-A, 2026-09-21) | FR-010 | recency refresh on overwrite (A8) |\n| R03 | FR-001, FR-011, FR-012 | maxsize validation, atomic replacement, one slot per value |\n| Preservation | FR-013 | existing APIs unchanged; built on LRUCache |\n| R04 (milestone 2) | FR-014, FR-015, FR-016 | invalidate_many any/all, empty=zero, fail-fast |\n| R05 (milestone 2) | FR-017, FR-018 | resize validation, LRU shrink, grow, maxsize property |\n| R05 clarification (round 2, 2026-09-22) | FR-019 | absolute-expiry preservation (binding when R07/R08 land; A10) |\n| R06 (milestone 2) | FR-020, FR-021 | generator tags consumed once; repeated detachment |\n| Conflicting design note | A9 | superseded by R04/R05; MUST NOT be relied upon |\n"}